; regression test for a real parsing bug found while writing tutorial/47-worked-proof.kurt: a
; blank line inside an indented block used to be read literally by the indentation tracker as
; "dedent all the way back to column 0" (a blank line's own leading-space count is always 0),
; incorrectly closing every currently open block instead of being ignored -- either raising a
; confusing `ParseError: expected increased indentation` (if the blank line came right after a
; block-opening keyword still expecting its first indented line) or, worse, silently closing
; blocks early and skipping real content that was never actually parsed (if the blank line came
; after an ordinary statement mid-block). Fixed in `read_eval_loop`: a blank line (and not
; already mid-statement) is now skipped outright, never fed through the indentation logic.
bool A, B

; case 1: blank line immediately after a block-opening keyword, before its first real line
show A implies A
proof

    assume A
        A
qed

; case 2: blank line between two ordinary statements inside a nested block
show B implies B
proof
    assume B

        B
    B implies B
qed

; case 3: blank line right after an `expect` block that just confirmed an error -- this is
; the exact shape that originally surfaced the bug (expect pops its own level on confirming,
; leaving the *parent* block's indentation still in force when the blank line is next)
use A
show B implies A
proof
    assume B
        expect "ProofError"
            false

        A
    B implies A
qed
